Generate a complete lesson plan and chapter assessment for the following English chapter.

=== CHAPTER SUMMARY (JSON, two-axis: main_sections × spines) ===
{
  "subject": "english",
  "stage": "middle",
  "grade": "vi",
  "chapter_number": 1,
  "chapter_title": "A Bottle of Dew (Fables and Folk Tales)",
  "main_sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "A",
      "title": "A Bottle of Dew",
      "type": "prose",
      "page_range": "p.1-12",
      "page_count": 12,
      "prose_summary": "A Bottle of Dew is a folk tale by Sudha Murty set in a rural Indian context. Rama Natha, the son of a rich landlord, inherits large tracts of land but neglects them entirely because he is obsessed with finding a magic potion that can turn any object into gold. He spends his time and money chasing this fantasy, while his wife Madhumati grows increasingly worried about their finances. When a famous sage named Mahipati visits their town, Rama Natha immediately seeks his counsel about the potion. The sage, to Rama Natha's astonishment, claims to know how to make it: Rama Natha must plant banana plants, water them himself, and each winter collect the morning dew from the leaves by hand. When he has gathered five litres of dew, he must bring it to the sage to be enchanted. Concerned that this would take years, Rama Natha asks if he can plant more plants — the sage agrees but insists Rama Natha must do all the work himself. Rama Natha returns home and, talking it over with Madhumati, begins cleaning and cultivating his neglected fields. He plants rows of banana plants, tends them carefully, and every winter painstakingly collects the dew. Madhumati partners with him — she gathers the banana crop, sells it at the market, and earns good money. Over six years, they build a huge banana plantation, and Rama Natha finally collects his five litres of dew. He brings the bottle to the sage, who mutters over it and returns it, asking him to try it. When Rama Natha sprinkles the dew on a copper vessel and nothing turns to gold, he feels cheated. At that moment, Madhumati steps forward carrying a large box of gold coins — the wealth accumulated over six years of honest work. The sage reveals that there was never a magic potion; the story was a ruse to make Rama Natha work the land himself. His hard work, combined with Madhumati's enterprise in selling the fruit, created the wealth — not magic. The tale closes with Rama Natha deeply understanding this wisdom and resolving to work even harder.",
      "spines": {
        "reading_for_comprehension": {
          "section_name": "Let us read + Let us discuss + Let us think and reflect",
          "tasks_verbatim": [
            {
              "task_text": "Let us discuss (after Part I): Answer the following questions.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "What did Rama Natha believe?",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.3"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "How did the sage help Rama Natha?",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.3"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Do you think Rama Natha will be able to collect the dew? Give a reason.",
                  "type": "ECR",
                  "page_ref": "p.3"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Let us discuss (after Part II): Answer the following questions.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Why was Rama Natha angry?",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.5"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "How did Rama Natha and Madhumati create wealth?",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.5"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Let us think and reflect I: Read the following lines and answer the questions that follow.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Complete the sentence with a suitable reason. Rama Natha did not 'give up' because _____________________.",
                  "type": "FILL_IN",
                  "page_ref": "p.6"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Read the column which shows 'what happened' and write the correct outcome in the next column.",
                  "type": "MATCH",
                  "table": "What Happened|Outcome\nA. People promised to tell Rama Natha about the magic potion.|\nB. Rama Natha was spending a lot of money.|",
                  "page_ref": "p.6"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Write whether the following statements are True or False. A. Madhumati was troubled about what her husband did. B. Rama Natha was very happy digging his fields. C. The sage wanted to show the right path to Rama Natha.",
                  "type": "TRUE_FALSE",
                  "page_ref": "p.6"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Choose the option that lists the events given below in the correct order. A. The sage smiled. B. Rama Natha gave the bottle to the sage. C. Rama Natha dropped a few drops of water on a copper vessel. D. He said something in a low voice over the water. E. Rama Natha waited to see if the magic worked. (a) A, E, D, B, C  (b) B, A, D, C, E  (c) C, D, A, B, E  (d) A, D, B, E, C",
                  "type": "MCQ",
                  "options": [
                    "A, E, D, B, C",
                    "B, A, D, C, E",
                    "C, D, A, B, E",
                    "A, D, B, E, C"
                  ],
                  "page_ref": "p.7"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Fill in the blank with the correct word from the lines given above. Seeing that the plant had dried up, the gardener ____________ some water on it.",
                  "type": "FILL_IN",
                  "page_ref": "p.7"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "How might Rama Natha have felt when nothing happened to the copper vessel?",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.7"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Let us think and reflect II: Answer the following questions.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "What did the sage ask Rama Natha to do to make the magic potion?",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.7"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Why did the sage ask Rama Natha to do everything himself?",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.7"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "How could Rama Natha have a big banana plantation after six years?",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.7"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "How did the sage make Rama Natha believe that there is no magic potion?",
                  "type": "SCR",
                  "page_ref": "p.7"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Fill in the 'before' and 'after' table about Rama Natha.",
                  "type": "MATCH",
                  "table": "What he did before meeting the sage|What he did after meeting the sage\n(i)|(i)\n(ii)|(ii)",
                  "page_ref": "p.7"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "listening": {
          "section_name": "Let us listen",
          "transcript_ref": "p.36",
          "transcript_text": "Madhumati is very happy as she has many big bags of bananas. Firstly, she visits her mother and gives her two dozen bananas. Secondly, she goes to the market to sell the bananas. Next, she meets her old friend Kalawati in the market. Then, she gifts Kalawati a dozen bananas. Later, she decides to go home as she has sold most of the bananas. After that, on the way home, she meets a little boy who is hungry and gives him two bananas. Lastly, she goes to her neighbour's house and gives them a bunch of bananas. She has a smile on her face as she goes home.",
          "tasks_verbatim": [
            {
              "task_text": "You will listen to what Madhumati does with the bananas from her plantation. As you listen, number the events in the correct order in which they happen. Write the number in the box beside each event.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Gives two bananas to a boy who is hungry",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.10"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Meets her friend Kalawati in the market",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.10"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Visits her mother and gives her two dozen bananas",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.10"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Chooses to go home as only some bananas were left",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.10"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Visits her neighbour and gifts them a bunch of bananas",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.10"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Gives twelve bananas to her friend",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.10"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Goes to the market to sell the bananas",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.10"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "speaking": {
          "section_name": "Let us speak",
          "tasks_verbatim": [
            {
              "task_text": "Read aloud the following words from the story: sage, promising, wisdom, trees, surprise, beds, sand, clothes, sea, was, keys, bags, nose. All these words have the letter 's' in them. But the sound of letter 's' is not always the same. In sage and promising the letter 's' has a /s/ sound; in wisdom and trees the letter 's' has a /z/ sound; in surprise the first letter 's' has a /s/ sound and the second has a /z/ sound. Now, speak each word clearly along with your teacher and classmates. Your teacher will now say some words. Listen carefully to the sound of 's'. Write the sound next to the word.",
              "question_bank": []
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Discuss the following questions with a partner. You may use the hints given in brackets while speaking. 1. What does hard work mean to you? (It means … / It is something … / Well, you see … /What I mean is …) 2. Give three reasons why you think hard work is important. (Firstly, I feel … because … / Secondly, I think … since … /Lastly, I believe … as … ) 3. Share three ideas you would give to someone who needs to work hard. (To begin with, it is important that … /Next, you could … / Finally, I think you should … )",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "What does hard work mean to you?",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.11"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Give three reasons why you think hard work is important.",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.11"
                },
                {
                  "stem": "Share three ideas you would give to someone who needs to work hard.",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.11"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "writing": {
          "section_name": "Let us write",
          "tasks_verbatim": [
            {
              "task_text": "Work in pairs to complete the description of a banana. You may take the help of the words given in brackets (covering: when I like to eat a banana, smell, healthy because, how the outer covering feels on touch, taste, colour of the outer covering and fruit). Now, use the information given in the picture to write eight sentences about a banana. Share your responses with your classmates and teacher.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Write eight sentences about a banana using the word-map clues provided.",
                  "type": "WRITING_TASK",
                  "page_ref": "p.11-12"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "On the basis of the story 'A Bottle of Dew' develop a script with dialogues and enact it with expressions. Present it in the assembly or your classroom.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Develop a script with dialogues based on 'A Bottle of Dew' and enact it.",
                  "type": "WRITING_TASK",
                  "page_ref": "p.12"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "vocabulary_grammar": {
          "section_name": "Let us learn",
          "tasks_verbatim": [
            {
              "task_text": "Read the following words from the story: promised, spending, cheated, luck, remember, huge, difficult, market. Use the words correctly to complete the paragraph given below. There are two extra words in the box that you do not need.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "There was a farmer who had a ________ coconut plantation. He worked hard and did not depend on ________. Every day was ________ but he did not give up. One day, a man bought a bag of coconuts from him, and ________ to pay him the money the next day. Days passed and the man did not pay him anything. The farmer felt ________. About two months later, he met the same man and asked him about his money. The man said, 'Oh! I had forgotten. Now, I ________ buying coconuts from you.' And he paid the money.",
                  "type": "FILL_IN",
                  "page_ref": "p.8"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Read the words from the story in Column A. Read the new words in Column B. Complete the meanings of the words given in Column B. The words given in each row have the same sounds but different spellings and meanings. Such words are called homophones.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Complete the meanings of the Column B homophones: eye (meaning of I), due (meaning of dew), sun (meaning of son), eight (meaning of ate), sea (meaning of see).",
                  "type": "MATCH",
                  "table": "Column A|Column B|Meaning of words in Column B\nI|eye|\nyour|you're|short form of 'you are'\ndew|due|happening at some time in the future\nson|sun|\none|won|past tense of win\nate|eight|\nsee|sea|",
                  "page_ref": "p.8"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Choose four pairs of homophones from the table above, and for each pair write a sentence that uses both homophones in the same sentence. Check your answers by sharing them with your teacher and classmates. An example has been given: I ate eight bananas for breakfast today.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Write four sentences each using a pair of homophones from the table.",
                  "type": "WRITING_TASK",
                  "page_ref": "p.9"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Match the phrases in Column A with the phrases in Column B. Use the connecting words given in the middle column (because, before, as soon as, as) to make meaningful sentences. Now, write the new sentences in your notebook.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Match: The children submitted their work (When?) — the bell rang. / He did not want to try rock climbing (Why?) — there was a traffic jam. / The children ran towards the gate (When?) — the deadline. / Nitin was late to school today (Why?) — of his fear of heights.",
                  "type": "MATCH",
                  "table": "Column A|Connecting Words|Column B\nThe children submitted their work (When?)|because / before / as soon as / as|the bell rang.\nHe did not want to try rock climbing (Why?)||there was a traffic jam.\nThe children ran towards the gate (When?)||the deadline.\nNitin was late to school today (Why?)||of his fear of heights.",
                  "page_ref": "p.9"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "task_text": "Here are two sentences with letter 'b'. Read them slowly the first time. As you read them a few more times, read them faster each time. 'Bunty bhaiya bought a big bunch of bananas.' 'A big bunch of bananas was bought by Bunty bhaiya.' Now, try to create a tongue twister on your own and share it with your peers.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Create your own tongue twister and share it with your peers.",
                  "type": "ORAL_PROMPT",
                  "page_ref": "p.9"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "beyond_text": {
          "section_name": "Let us explore",
          "tasks_verbatim": [
            {
              "task_text": "Find out the varieties of bananas which are grown, cultivated and eaten in different parts of India. You can find information about them from websites such as: https://nhb.gov.in/pdf/fruits/banana/ban013",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Research and find out the varieties of bananas grown, cultivated, and eaten across different parts of India.",
                  "type": "PROJECT",
                  "page_ref": "p.12"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "competency_reporting": {
    "by_spine": {
      "reading_for_comprehension": [
        "C-1.1",
        "C-2.1",
        "C-2.2"
      ],
      "listening": [
        "C-1.1",
        "C-1.2"
      ],
      "speaking": [
        "C-1.2",
        "C-1.3",
        "C-2.3"
      ],
      "writing": [
        "C-1.4",
        "C-1.5",
        "C-2.3",
        "C-3.2"
      ],
      "vocabulary_grammar": [
        "C-3.1",
        "C-5.1",
        "C-5.2",
        "C-5.3"
      ],
      "beyond_text": [
        "C-2.1",
        "C-4.2"
      ]
    }
  },
  "effort_signals": {
    "spine_load": 3,
    "task_density": 2,
    "writing_demand": 1,
    "project_load": 0,
    "effort_index": 10.5
  },
  "_source_unit": {
    "unit_chapter_number": 1,
    "unit_chapter_title": "Fables and Folk Tales",
    "section_id": "A",
    "page_count": 12,
    "page_share_of_unit": 0.3429
  }
}

=== TEACHER PERIOD SCHEDULE ===
Period schedule:
  Row 1: 40 minutes × 5 periods = 200 minutes
Total: 5 periods · 3h 20min

=== INSTRUCTIONS ===
Follow the English LP Constitution and Assessment Constitution exactly.
Produce a SINGLE valid JSON object with this top-level structure:

{
  "grade": "Grade VI",
  "subject": "English",
  "stage": "middle",
  "chapter_number": 1,
  "chapter_title": "A Bottle of Dew (Fables and Folk Tales)",
  "period_schedule": <derived from teacher period schedule above>,

  "main_sections_inventory": [
    { "section_id": "A|B|C", "title": "...", "type": "prose|poem|narrative|dialogue|informational" }
  ],

  "periods_allocated": <integer = total period count from the teacher schedule>,

  "lesson_plan": {
    "periods": [
      <one object per period per LP Constitution Rule 1+2 — each period
       anchors to ONE main_section + 1-2 spines within it; periods walk
       main_sections in textbook order then spines within each section.
       Required fields: period_number, period_duration_minutes,
       section_id, section_title, spines_taught, activity_title,
       pedagogical_methods (object keyed by each spine in spines_taught;
       each value is one method drawn from that spine's permitted list
       in LP Rule 4 for the stage — keys MUST equal spines_taught
       exactly), tasks_in_class (each {spine, task_index, task_brief}),
       homework, time_bands (each {minutes, activity}; tile 0..duration
       with no gaps), teacher_notes
       (2-3 sentences max, grounded in main_section's prose_summary or
       poem_appreciation_summary), materials.>
    ]
  },

  "coverage_handoff": {
    "reading_for_comprehension": { "section_contributions": [<contribution>] },
    "listening":                 { "section_contributions": [...] },
    "speaking":                  { "section_contributions": [...] },
    "writing":                   { "section_contributions": [...] },
    "vocabulary_grammar":        { "section_contributions": [...] },
    "beyond_text":               { "section_contributions": [...] }
  },

  "assessment_items": [
    {
      "spine_code":  "reading_for_comprehension|listening|speaking|writing|vocabulary_grammar|beyond_text",
      "spine_title": "Reading for Comprehension|Listening|Speaking|Writing|Vocabulary and Grammar|Beyond the Text",
      "note":        "",
      "items": [
        <the items Assessment Constitution Rule 2 requires for each
         section_contribution in coverage_handoff for this spine — read
         Rule 2 and its slot table for the count and the slot order; do
         not assume one. Every item tests the cell's implied_lo, and a
         cell's items sample it at DIFFERENT rungs.

         STRICT GENERATION RULES — these override everything else:
         - DO NOT read summary.<spine>.tasks_verbatim[] or question_bank[].
           These fields are FORBIDDEN inputs to the assessment generator.
           Reading either field is a constitution violation regardless of
           intent. The implied_lo in coverage_handoff already encodes
           what was taught.
         - Derive every item solely from the section's content sources
           (prose_summary (prose/informational sections) or poem_text + poem_appreciation_summary (poem sections)) plus the implied_lo from
           coverage_handoff.
         - The item MUST be original — it must not reproduce, paraphrase,
           or structurally echo any textbook exercise wording.
         - The item MUST be visibly grounded in the section's actual
           content: name a character, scene, specific line, grammar
           concept, or writing context drawn from prose_summary /
           poem_text. Generic questions that could apply to any chapter
           are prohibited (Assessment Rule 3).

         Required fields per item: id (e.g. "Q-RFC-A-1"),
         source_section_id, source_section_title, source_section_type,
         source_spine, source_lo (implied_lo copied verbatim from
         coverage_handoff), item_stem (original question grounded in
         section content), question_type (from Assessment Rule 4 set:
         MCQ|SCR|ECR|MATCH|FILL_IN|TRUE_FALSE|ORAL_PROMPT|WRITING_TASK|PROJECT), options ([] unless MCQ or TRUE_FALSE),
         visual_stimulus ("" or pipe-table only), transcript_ref
         (Listening items only; "" otherwise), teacher_guide
         {suggested_answer (CLOSED non-MCQ items; "" otherwise),
         expected_elements (OPEN items: 3-4 bullets ≤ 12
         words each; [] otherwise), note ("" unless fallback)},
         verified (true for open items; true for closed only when
         answer is unambiguously supported by the section's content
         sources).>
      ]
    }
  ]
}

CRITICAL CONSTRAINTS:
- Total LP period count = the teacher schedule's period_count. Distribute
  across (section × spine) cells in textbook order (LP Rule 1+2), with
  per-section period share roughly proportional to the section's
  page_count (±1 period tolerance).
- Total assessment item count is set by ASSESSMENT RULE 2, applied to
  every section_contribution across all spines in coverage_handoff that
  has at least one anchored task. Rule 2 and its slot table are the only
  authority on how many items a cell carries and in what order they are
  emitted — read them; do not assume one per cell. Spines with no
  section_contributions are omitted entirely. For each item, read ONLY
  the cell's implied_lo from coverage_handoff and the section's content
  sources (prose_summary / poem_text + poem_appreciation_summary).
  DO NOT read tasks_verbatim[] or question_bank[] for any purpose —
  these fields are forbidden inputs to the assessment generator.
  Every item must be original and grounded in the section content.
- C-codes MUST NOT appear anywhere in the LP or assessment JSON.
- `pedagogical_methods` per period MUST be an object whose keys equal
  `spines_taught` exactly. Each value MUST be drawn from that spine's
  permitted method list in LP Constitution Rule 4 for the middle
  stage. Do NOT invent methods. Do NOT collapse multiple spines onto
  a single method.
- Listening items: transcript_ref format is "p.NN" (transcript inside the chapter PDF). The summary carries the value verbatim.
- The answer layer applies per item. A closed item (MCQ, FILL_IN,
  MATCH, TRUE_FALSE, factual SCR) carries
  `teacher_guide.suggested_answer` (verified against the section's
  content sources; omitted for MCQ — correct option is flagged in
  options[].is_correct). An open item (ORAL_PROMPT, WRITING_TASK, PROJECT, ECR, reflective SCR) carries
  `teacher_guide.expected_elements` (3-4 short bullets,
  each ≤ 12 words). No item carries both fields.

LENGTH CONSTRAINTS:
- Each time band `activity`: 2-3 sentences maximum.
- Each `teacher_notes`: 2-3 sentences maximum.
- Each `suggested_answer`: 1-2 sentences plain prose.
- Each `expected_elements` bullet: ≤ 12 words.

Output only the raw JSON object. No markdown. No prose. No headers. No ```json fences.

Generate a complete lesson plan and chapter assessment for the following English chapter.

=== CHAPTER SUMMARY (JSON, two-axis: main_sections × spines) ===
{
  "subject": "english",
  "stage": "middle",
  "grade": "vi",
  "chapter_number": 2,
  "chapter_title": "The Raven and the Fox (Fables and Folk Tales)",
  "main_sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "B",
      "title": "The Raven and the Fox",
      "type": "poem",
      "page_range": "p.13-19",
      "page_count": 7,
      "poem_text": "Mr Raven was perched upon a limb,\nAnd Reynard the Fox looked up at him;\nFor the Raven held in his great big beak,\nA morsel the Fox would go far to seek.\n\nSaid the Fox, in admiring tones: \"My word!\nSir Raven, you are a handsome bird.\nSuch feathers! If you would only sing,\nThe birds of these woods would call you King.\"\n\nThe Raven, who did not see the joke,\nForgot that his voice was just a croak.\nHe opened his beak, in his foolish pride–\nAnd down fell the morsel the Fox had eyed.\n\n\"Ha-ha!\" laughed the Fox. \"And now you know,\nIgnore sweet words that make you glow.\nPride, my friend, is rather unwise;\nI'm sure this teaching is quite a surprise.\"\n\nJean de La Fontaine",
      "poem_appreciation_summary": "This four-stanza poem is a classic beast fable adapted from Jean de La Fontaine's version of the Aesop tale. The central theme is the danger of vanity and pride. A raven perched on a branch clutches a tasty morsel of food. A cunning fox below flatters the raven with praise about his looks and feathers, then suggests that if only the raven would sing, he would be crowned king of the forest. The raven, blinded by his pride, opens his beak to sing and drops his food. The fox takes it and departs with a moralising laugh: pride is unwise, and one should not be swayed by flattery. The dominant literary devices are rhyming couplets (limb/him, beak/seek, word/bird, etc.) and alliteration (great big beak, song of sweet birds). The tone shifts from admiring flattery in stanzas one and two to ironic triumph in stanza four. The moral — that excessive pride makes one vulnerable to manipulation — is made explicit by the fox himself in the final stanza.",
      "spines": {
        "reading_for_comprehension": {
          "section_name": "Let us read + Let us discuss + Let us think and reflect",
          "tasks_verbatim": [
            {
              "task_text": "Let us discuss I: Fill in the blanks by choosing the correct option, stanza by stanza.",
              "question_bank": [
                {
                  "stem": "Stanza 1: (i) Mr Raven is a __________. (bird/frog) (ii) The name of the fox is __________. (Raven/Reynard) (iii) Mr Raven had __________ in his beak. (a piece of food/a bunch of leaves)",
                  "type": "MCQ",
                  "options": [
                    "bird",
                    "Reynard",
                    "a piece of food"
                  ],
                  "page_ref": "p.14"
