      "prose_summary": "Jahnavi is a bright girl of nearly ten living in a coastal village in India. Her parents work on the farm, and she has been kept home to mind her younger siblings — first Ramu, then little Appu — while her brother Gopi (whom she calls Ettan, meaning 'Elder brother') and neighbour Meena attend school. Sitting by the river one day, weeping silently as a kingfisher swoops and a lizard basks, Jahnavi hears a sleepy, murmuring voice that turns out to be the River itself. The River, gently personified throughout, draws out her sorrow: she has never been allowed to go to school, is frightened it is too late, and yet burns with curiosity — she wants to know why spiders are yellow in yellow flowers, why bamboo trees rustle, why the moon follows the same path, why fish in the field turn to frogs. The River, with quiet wit, reminds her that little girls can do as much as little boys, and advises her simply to slip into the school one morning and see what happens. Jahnavi summons courage and does exactly that, creeping into the back row with sleeping Appu on her shoulder. The teacher notices her, learns she is Gopi's sister, and promises to speak to her father. That evening Jahnavi watches the teacher visit their home; her father scratches his cheek in worry while the teacher persuades him. Her mother, who once longed for school herself but was denied by her own mother, warmly supports Jahnavi's wish, saying girls should learn as much as they want. Jahnavi resolves that when she grows up she will be a teacher, going house to house to bring all the village girls to school. The next morning she runs to the river to share her triumph — 'I did it! I was scared, but I did it!' — and hears the river chuckle and promise to tell her all about the ships that sail the sea. The story is an extract adapted from Kamala Nair's 'The Day the River Spoke'. Its central themes are the right to education, gender equality, self-belief, and the power of nature as both witness and counsel.",
