      "prose_summary": "The Tunnel, written by Ruskin Bond, is a short story set in an Indian jungle. Suraj, a curious and adventurous boy, cycles out to a railway tunnel cut through a forested hill. He watches a steam engine roar through, and then walks the tunnel's dark, smoky interior just to experience it. On the hillside beyond, he glimpses a flash of orange and gold — a leopard. He meets the tunnel's sole watchman, Sunder Singh, an old man who feels a proprietary affection for the tunnel, the jungle, and even the leopard that periodically visits the area. Sunder Singh invites Suraj to return that evening to watch the night mail pass through.\n\nSuraj comes back at dusk. The two sit together, drinking tea and listening to the forest's nighttime sounds — a barking deer, a fox, a nightjar, the creaking of trees. Sunder Singh makes his lamp-lit inspection of the tunnel. Inside, he hears something: a padded footfall, rustling leaves. He suspects the leopard is in the tunnel. With the night mail due in minutes, they cannot let it be killed. Suraj, despite his dry throat and fear, follows Sunder Singh into the dark. Their combined shouting drives the leopard out. The train thunders through, and the moment is exhilarating.\n\nThe story closes a week later: Suraj is on the night mail itself, accompanying his father to Delhi. In the darkness, he glimpses the distant lamp of Sunder Singh's hut and feels a quiet, warm connection to his friend — the lonely watchman who lights the dark for trains and leopards alike. The story's themes are curiosity, courage, unlikely friendship between generations, and a reverence for jungle life. Bond's prose captures the sensory richness of forest sounds, the power of a steam engine, and the intimacy of two people in darkness doing a brave, small thing together.",
