      "prose_summary": "The story is adapted from Donald J. Sobol's Encyclopedia Brown series and centres on Leroy Brown, an exceptionally gifted eighth-grader nicknamed 'Encyclopedia' because of his vast, encyclopaedic knowledge. His father, Chief Brown, is the head of the Idaville Police Department, and their usual practice is for the father to share unsolved cases at the dinner table, where Leroy quietly solves them before the meal ends. The story opens on a Tuesday evening when Chief Brown is visibly troubled. He explains that Tim Nolan, a man long suspected of involvement in a million-dollar jewellery heist from the Diamond Mart five years earlier, has died of a stroke. During the robbery, one of two masked men—believed to be Nolan and his accomplice Daniel Davenport—had his mask slip, but the clerk was not certain enough to testify. No stolen jewellery was ever found, and Davenport disappeared after the crime. That morning, officers had searched Nolan's property—a small palm-tree nursery—and found nothing.\n\nThe twist lies in the document found clipped to Nolan's dying will: a sheet from a desk calendar bearing Davenport's name and address, and four cryptic words—'Nom Utes Sweden Hurts'. Chief Brown and Mrs. Brown try to decode the words independently. Mrs. Brown identifies their literal meanings (nominative, an American Indian tribe, a European country, and the verb hurts) but cannot piece together a hidden message. Leroy, however, silently analyses the code. He asks one perfectly targeted question: Is there a young fir tree in Nolan's nursery? When his father confirms there is one on the south side, Leroy reveals that each word is a day of the week with the letters 'd-a-y' removed—Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday—and the unwritten fifth word (the hiding place) is 'Fir', standing for Friday. The stolen jewellery is discovered inside a twenty-gallon jug of earth in which the young fir tree grows. The story celebrates lateral thinking, the power of pattern recognition, and the quiet modesty of true intelligence—Leroy never boasts, choosing instead to blend in with ordinary boys.",
