      "prose_summary": "The Cherry Tree is a tender narrative by Ruskin Bond that traces the bond between a young boy, Rakesh, and a cherry tree he grows from a single seed over several years in the Himalayan foothills near Mussoorie. Rakesh, who lives with his grandfather while attending school, walks home one day eating cherries bought from the Mussoorie bazaar. On his grandfather's gentle prompting that luck must be put to use, he plants the last cherry seed in a soft, shaded corner of the garden. He then forgets about it entirely, absorbed in the rhythms of school and cricket. Spring brings the first surprise — a tiny rooted shoot that Rakesh mistakes for a twig before joyfully calling his grandfather. The two bend together in wonder over a four-inch sapling. Seasons accumulate around the growing tree: monsoon rains speed its growth to two feet, only for a wayward goat to strip all its leaves; a grass-cutter's scythe slices it in two; field-mice cluster in the cottage roof under winter snow while the tree bends low under its weight. Yet each time the cherry tree recovers, sending out new shoots with quiet determination, mirroring Rakesh's own steady growth from a six-year-old boy to a sturdy nine-year-old. One February morning, grandfather spots the first pale pink blossom and calls out in disbelief and delight. In subsequent years blossoms multiply; bees feed on nectar; bulbuls and scarlet minivets feast on small cherries; a praying-mantis and a hairy caterpillar arrive as uninvited guests. Eventually grandfather finds the perfect shade of the fully grown tree, reclining in a cane chair and watching leaves dance in the breeze, while Rakesh lies beneath it gazing up at the blue sky and distant mountains. In the dusk, with crickets and cicadas filling the forest, Rakesh asks why this tree is special among so many. His grandfather answers simply: 'We planted it ourselves.' Touching the smooth bark, Rakesh whispers, 'Is this what it feels to be God?' The story celebrates patience, the quiet triumph of nature, and the deep human satisfaction of nurturing life from a single seed.",
