      "poem_text": "Men's Voices:\nLord of the lotus, lord of the harvest,\nBright and munificent lord of the morn!\nThine is the bounty that prospered our sowing,\nThine is the bounty that nurtured our corn.\nWe bring thee our songs and our garlands for tribute,\nThe gold of our fields and the gold of our fruit;\nO giver of mellowing radiance, we hail thee,\nWe praise thee, O Surya, with cymbal and flute.\n\nLord of the rainbow, lord of the harvest,\nGreat and beneficent lord of the main!\nThine is the mercy that cherished our furrows,\nThine is the mercy that fostered our grain.\nWe bring thee our thanks and our garlands for tribute,\nThe wealth of our valleys, new-garnered and ripe;\nO sender of rain and the dewfall, we hail thee,\nWe praise thee, Varuna, with cymbal and pipe.\n\nWomen's Voices:\nQueen of the gourd-flower, queen of the harvest,\nSweet and omnipotent mother, O Earth!\nThine is the plentiful bosom that feeds us,\nThine is the womb where our riches have birth.\nWe bring thee our love and our garlands for tribute,\nWith gifts of thy opulent giving we come;\nO source of our manifold gladness, we hail thee,\nWe praise thee, O Prithvi, with cymbal and drum.\n\nAll Voices:\nLord of the Universe, Lord of our being,\nFather eternal, ineffable Om!\nThou art the Seed and the Scythe of our harvests,\nThou art our Hands and our Heart and our Home.\nWe bring thee our lives and our labours for tribute,\nGrant us thy succour, thy counsel, thy care.\nO Life of all life and all blessing, we hail thee,\nWe praise thee, O Bramha, with cymbal and prayer.",
      "poem_appreciation_summary": "Harvest Hymn by Sarojini Naidu is a devotional ode of gratitude offered by the farming community at harvest time. Structured as four stanzas sung by distinct voices — men, women, and all together — the hymn addresses three elemental deities: Surya (the Sun), Varuna (the Rain), and Prithvi (the Earth), before culminating in an address to Brahma, the universal creator. The central theme is the deep, reverent interdependence between farmers and the forces of nature that sustain life. The tone is celebratory and humble simultaneously — the speakers bring songs, garlands, the literal gold of their fields, and ultimately their own lives as tribute. Naidu employs rich imagery of light, moisture, seed, and harvest; anaphora ('Thine is... Thine is') creates a litany-like rhythm that reinforces collective devotion. The dominant metaphor in the final stanza — God as both Seed and Scythe — encapsulates the Hindu concept of the divine as the source and end of all creation. Personification of Earth as an all-nourishing mother is especially vivid. The poem's musical quality, achieved through repeated refrains ('we hail thee... we praise thee') and the mention of cymbals, flute, pipe, drum, and prayer, recreates the sensory atmosphere of a harvest festival.",
